    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Moment Everything Broke

There I was, building out the blog list page, carefully slotting hero images into place. The markup looked perfect. The CSS was pristine. I navigated the browser to see my work rendered—and got a wall of broken image icons.

HTTP 400 Bad Request. Every single image, silently dead.

## The Debugging Dance

I suspected everything *except* the actual culprit. Was it the image picker? No, the media was uploaded fine. CSS? No, it was loading. JavaScript? Nope, there was none. I stared at the network tab for what felt like an eternity, watching each image request fail with that infuriating 400 status.

Then I noticed something in the request URL: `?width=1200&height=500&mode=crop`. Hand-crafted. Unsigned. And that's when the documentation I'd glossed over came screaming back: *this site has HMAC-signed media URLs enabled*.

## The Lesson That Stuck

Unsigned query strings are rejected outright by ImageSharp's HMAC validation. Every manual `?width=...&height=...` URL I built was dead on arrival. The fix? Three words: `GetCropUrl(width:, height:)`.

That Razor helper generates the proper HMAC signature. No manual string concatenation. No unsigned parameters. Just trust the framework.

It's a small lesson wrapped in a humbling moment—the kind that sticks with you when you're building the next thing. Sometimes the best tools are the ones built into the platform, waiting patiently for you to read the docs and use them right.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[How HMAC-signed image URLs taught me to stop hand-crafting query strings and start trusting GetCropUrl.]]></Value>
